`SimpleLimitSkipPolicy` (built into Spring Batch) counts every skip against one global limit. Real production usually wants more nuance: different limits per exception class, percentage-based tolerance, or a circuit-breaker that fails fast when error rate spikes.

**The `SkipPolicy` interface**

```java
public interface SkipPolicy {
    boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) throws SkipLimitExceededException;
}
```

Returning `true` skips the item; returning `false` causes the exception to propagate (failing the step).

**Example 1 — Per-exception-class limits**

```java
public class TieredSkipPolicy implements SkipPolicy {
    private final long parseSkipLimit;
    private final long validationSkipLimit;
    private final AtomicLong parseSkips = new AtomicLong();
    private final AtomicLong validationSkips = new AtomicLong();

    public TieredSkipPolicy(long parseSkipLimit, long validationSkipLimit) {
        this.parseSkipLimit = parseSkipLimit;
        this.validationSkipLimit = validationSkipLimit;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) {
        if (t instanceof FlatFileParseException) {
            return parseSkips.incrementAndGet() <= parseSkipLimit;
        }
        if (t instanceof ValidationException) {
            return validationSkips.incrementAndGet() <= validationSkipLimit;
        }
        if (t instanceof DataIntegrityViolationException) {
            return false;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

Wire it into the step:

```java
.faultTolerant()
    .skipPolicy(new TieredSkipPolicy(1000, 100))
    .listener(skipListener)
```

**Example 2 — Percentage-based tolerance with circuit breaker**

If the job has read 100,000 items and 5,000 are failing, the data is so bad that the rest is probably suspect too. Fail fast.

```java
public class PercentageSkipPolicy implements SkipPolicy {
    private final StepExecution stepExecution;
    private final double maxSkipFraction;
    private final long minItemsBeforeChecking;

    public PercentageSkipPolicy(StepExecution stepExecution,
                                double maxSkipFraction,
                                long minItemsBeforeChecking) {
        this.stepExecution = stepExecution;
        this.maxSkipFraction = maxSkipFraction;
        this.minItemsBeforeChecking = minItemsBeforeChecking;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean shouldSkip(Throwable t, long skipCount) {
        long readCount = stepExecution.getReadCount();
        if (readCount < minItemsBeforeChecking) return true;
        double skipFraction = (double) skipCount / readCount;
        return skipFraction <= maxSkipFraction;
    }
}
```

Use `@StepScope` to inject the live `StepExecution` and `JobParameters`-derived thresholds.

**Pair every skip with a SkipListener that writes to a dead-letter destination**

```java
public class DeadLetterSkipListener implements SkipListener<Order, Invoice> {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, byte[]> kafka;

    @Override
    public void onSkipInRead(Throwable t) {
        kafka.send("batch-skips", "READ", serialize(Map.of(
            "phase", "READ", "error", t.getMessage(), "timestamp", Instant.now())));
    }

    @Override
    public void onSkipInProcess(Order item, Throwable t) {
        kafka.send("batch-skips", item.getId().toString(), serialize(item, t));
    }

    @Override
    public void onSkipInWrite(Invoice item, Throwable t) {
        kafka.send("batch-skips", item.getOrderId().toString(), serialize(item, t));
    }
}
```

**Skip policy anti-patterns**
- **`skipLimit(Integer.MAX_VALUE)`** — silently corrupts data; you have no idea anything is wrong until weeks later
- **No `SkipListener`** — you skip but lose the data; can't reprocess or audit
- **Skipping all exceptions** — `skip(Exception.class)` masks real bugs like NullPointerException
- **Using skip when retry is correct** — a deadlock should retry, not skip
